<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?>
<FinalDraft DocumentType="Script" Template="No" Version="6">

  <DocumentRef DateTime="19691231T185959" id="11959490-87e9-458a-bfda-d7d416088c58"/>

  <Content>
    <Paragraph Type="New Act" id="175b0579-f059-4b02-9867-6d5c232957cb">
      <Text>Safari</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="aa61c176-d160-4eea-8ef4-000af8ad9059">
      <Text>The </Text>
      <Text Style="AllCaps">Lioness</Text>
      <Text> prowls in with a feline grace. They address the audience as if they are the pride of lions who have shunned them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="d01575cf-7792-429b-8e29-68c1e4ae6ee7">
      <Text>Lioness</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical" id="d8b17591-7895-48e6-ae25-53fdf035e656">
      <Text>(Growl)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="c82ebee3-50f3-4ae2-899e-7199a0ee8004">
      <Text>Roar. Don’t look at me like that. I know what the safari says. They call me a monster. Like all lioness. Nature says that a lioness who does not hunt her meat, but washes their ears, has lost her mind.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="932a48bf-b281-4216-a9b2-2ef49e3837a5">
      <Text>Lioness crouches, with their eyes tracking a calf in the front row.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="bfcc3094-ed91-4ec1-8e8d-c954ec7023b2">
      <Text>Lioness</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="a9907c5d-0744-496d-898f-ec8ab607de3f">
      <Text>But look at this baby antelope. This little... foster. My first. They were shivering in the shade. Their mother ran... I do not blame her, I have a face like a nightmare... but the calf stayed. They looked at me and did not see a predator. They saw a shadow to hide in.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="a681e0c3-5deb-464a-a839-a0bda4f321b8">
      <Text>Lioness approaches an audience member.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="6eaa25ed-708f-4688-bc01-c01072027f47">
      <Text>Lioness</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="cb7f55a2-6fac-484e-9321-083e67d21c0f">
      <Text>You. Hold your hands out. Softly. That’s how I had to carry him. Not with teeth to eat, but with gums of fun. Tell me: if you are born to destroy, but choose to guard, are you still a monster? Or are you finally a mother?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="9d7eee9c-c16f-4bee-bd29-93309b57efb8">
      <Text>Lioness waits for a brief answer.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="4d6225e8-4f92-427d-a316-5d94a5d6af6a">
      <Text>Lioness</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical" id="aa4eee07-a58e-4e56-83ea-df0703d3fbe2">
      <Text>(Nodding)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="d0c16d5c-2064-4572-bd2f-d8725148ce05">
      <Text>The biologists, they sat in their Jeeps and scribbled in notebooks. “Lioness,” they called me. “The Blessed One.” They watched me starve because I would not leave my foster to hunt. I grew thin so he could grow tall.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="bc04b581-b2c5-430c-8a03-806a15d19296">
      <Text>
Then came my second foster calf. Then third. Six times the Great Spirit sent me a child with hooves instead of claws. Each time, the same story: the hunger in my belly screaming to eat them, and the heart in my chest telling me to feed them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="d0773d73-dae3-45bb-9c9f-36c777bda090">
      <Text>Lioness points to different sections of the audience.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="d07bb53d-3381-4363-a4c6-ea7786a2c1a9">
      <Text>Lioness</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="cda2ce36-3bdc-4eb4-a663-be32d9a14cf5">
      <Text>You over there... you leopards. Snarling in the dark. And you- you hyenas, waiting for me to sleep. I need help. Everyone, make the sound of the dry wind. Low and steady.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="b8508d76-1d3e-48f0-9db9-753aa1889478">
      <Text>Lioness stands center, arms spread wide as if shielding a small huddle.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="590db93b-2975-4132-ad4d-99fc62e07a3f">
      <Text>LIONESS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="bf63b67b-ea7c-479e-9e63-fb305e05a973">
      <Text>I fought the big males. I took scars on my flanks to keep my monsters safe. People think motherhood is a soft thing, all milk and licking. No. Motherhood is a war. It is a choice you make every sunrise: Today, nothing dies on my watch.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="2a650333-a81d-4033-9445-1650de0bd4a7">
      <Text>The wind fades. Lioness drops their arms, looking weary but peaceful.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="159ee012-9750-46ea-a96c-50185e426b65">
      <Text>Lioness</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="fd6896f4-4013-4aab-8c05-3925b64e30c2">
      <Text>However, the wild took them eventually. A mother lion cannot outrun the law of the bush forever. But for those weeks, the lions and the antelopes slept in the same dust.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="b9d5f4fc-f414-435c-8a6e-0dd0d6e12501">
      <Text>Lioness leans in close to an audience member.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="6e7a6708-b765-4ae4-8f5a-74a736364f70">
      <Text>Lioness</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="0080a384-89c7-4b07-9bb5-4607b514d933">
      <Text>If you had to protect something that was supposed to be your enemy... would you have the courage to be a monster like me?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="7942181b-0bfa-49eb-9f73-c3d6c25c4900">
      <Text>Lioness turns, walking slowly towards the back of the stage. They pause, looking over their shoulder with a regal gaze.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="9a3247f5-0422-4d12-8b7f-6fac6972b0cc">
      <Text>LIONESS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="456e52cd-fb04-4dc7-ac9d-ad5de439b665">
      <Text>I am the Lioness. I am the Blessed. And I am still hungry... but only for the peace of the nursery.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="New Act" id="45fbb978-9102-45f5-a9c9-5167299b1240">
      <Text>JeeP</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="d1da2d27-d244-47a2-a470-48ab4ab94464">
      <Text>An observer, wearing a field vest and clutching a notebook, looks out with binoculars.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="b2f37b89-7481-4aa6-8483-03b7df830ae7">
      <Text>Observer</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="fa7ad48d-b538-4a29-a340-7c63e9c57b52">
      <Text>Six times. Six times she chose starvation over the kill. I’ve spent twenty years tracking the </Text>
      <Text AdornmentStyle="-1">panthera</Text>
      <Text> </Text>
      <Text AdornmentStyle="-1">leo</Text>
      <Text> across the Samburu. I know their biology. I know the calory required to maintain a hundred-and-fifty-kilo predator. By all laws of nature, she should be dead.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="4f7e7a36-d225-4821-b02b-32dd7830d216">
      <Text>The observer paces, looking at a few audience members.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="b2f37b89-7481-4aa6-8483-03b7df830ae7">
      <Text>Observer</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="ca95e668-2afd-484e-8c8d-9e15394e7894">
      <Text>You. If I took away your grocery store, your kitchen, </Text>
      <Text AdornmentStyle="-1">DoorDash</Text>
      <Text>... and I handed you a sandwich. But then, I put a shivering baby deer next to you that needed that sandwich to survive. How many days before your stomach overrides your soul?
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="c2bf87d9-4f73-4735-8395-b419f5a26507">
      <Text>The data says day three. By day four, the instinct to survive drowns out everything else. But she... that Lioness is just messed up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="a713d244-ddc9-443f-bf6b-a18422de7782">
      <Text>The observer moves into the aisle, pointing to an audience member’s hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="b2f37b89-7481-4aa6-8483-03b7df830ae7">
      <Text>Observer</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="c783b0ec-cb96-4d87-8261-b2d198c14f8f">
      <Text>Look at your hands. We call this </Text>
      <Text Style="Italic">nurturing</Text>
      <Text>. We see it as a soft, mammalian trait. But in the bush, motherhood is a mathematical error. It’s an investment of energy with no guaranteed return.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical" id="57ba1556-84a5-4416-bf31-de11b4b3de9e">
      <Text>(to another audience member)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="7e735c96-598f-415b-9d2e-0abf5f6264dc">
      <Text>Help me with a calculation. Tell me a number: how many scars is one life worth? If you had to take a hit from a leopard to save something that wasn't even your own kind... what’s the limit?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="e8f66519-39ab-450c-a109-28fd0c989fe8">
      <Text>The observer listens, then sighs, closing the notebook with a heavy thud.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="705b9d03-f4f9-4f92-8ad3-886367cec0ea">
      <Text>Observer</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="a454107f-ab54-4ba7-ac7e-b1686fd91ca8">
      <Text>The Blessed One. We gave her that name cause she has no explanation to be. We keep looking for that motherhood gene, that oxytocin spike, that biological trigger. But maybe it’s not a trigger. Maybe it’s a double-edged sword.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="618e4294-6dd5-4a9d-be81-f17ae3674e37">
      <Text>The observer looks towards the back of the stage where </Text>
      <Text AdornmentStyle="-1">Lioness</Text>
      <Text> exited.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="1254bdc1-0f40-40e0-acf7-55c02d3b173e">
      <Text>Observer</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="69d23171-89df-485d-8e63-ae9f01bb3940">
      <Text>She is no monster like the rest of the lions. She’s a monster because she broke the only law the world has: Eat or be eaten.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="fa9f5c10-e461-46f5-9531-bc28b13e9afb">
      <Text>The observer looks back at the audience.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="5d2fba4e-97ae-47a6-b67c-93ca9279584c">
      <Text>Observer</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="45c6f484-4a3d-4b37-ac3a-cb6960e47637">
      <Text>I’m starting to think being a mother and being a predator are no different. Based on my observations, I came here to study a lioness. I think I’m just studying a miracle.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action" id="6b66f569-fae5-49cc-b171-0fc989639150">
      <Text>The observer steps on a centipede.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character" id="b199b23d-e854-48d2-ba1b-3925e98c843a">
      <Text>Observer</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue" id="9888cbc4-95a1-42d2-b2f7-518ca53a266e">
      <Text>Oh crap... I just stepped on a centipede. A mother centipede. She was feeding her babies. Oh, you don’t wanna know. There’s a process called matriphagy. Okay, long story short: the mother lets her babies eat her insides out. Well, it’s no different than what you mothers do anyways... Against, we’re around miracles. </Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="End of Act" id="a3172081-1aa2-4f43-8d7a-8ffcbf054f4c">
      <Text>Mother of God</Text>
    </Paragraph>
  </Content>

